<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Vicus Helena</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 448</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Vicus Helena was fought in the 440s, probably around 448, at an unidentified place in Artois in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. The Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> and the future emperor Majorian surprised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-franks">Salian Franks</a> of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlodio">Chlodio</a> during a wedding celebration and defeated them. The setback did not stop Frankish expansion, and Chlodio kept the lands he had taken up to the Somme.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-vicus-helena.jpg" alt="Battle of Vicus Helena" width="320" height="282" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:400_Germania_I_II_Belgica_I_II.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> were foederati of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, yet they regularly plundered towns and villages inside the empire. Some time between 445 and 450 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-franks">Salian Franks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlodio">Chlodio</a> took Turnacum (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a>) and Cameracum (Cambrai), which became centres of Frankish power, and then pushed towards the river Somme. Around 448 the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Nemetocenna</a> (Arras) was probably sacked as well, though it is unknown whether by Franks or by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>. Majorian, the general who became Western Roman emperor in 457, had just put down a revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagaudae">Bagaudae</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armorica">Armorica</a> and defended Turonum (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>) against a siege.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Most of what is known comes from the Panegyric to Majorian, written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidonius-apollinaris">Sidonius Apollinaris</a> in 458 to praise Majorian's campaigns, with some circumstantial detail in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>'s History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. Sidonius described a place where several roads narrowed into a defile, with the village of Vicus Helena forming an arc and a river crossed by a bridge of wooden planks. On a nearby hill the Franks were celebrating a wedding with singing and dancing when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> attacked.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Flavius Aetius</a> directed the operation while Majorian fought with the cavalry at the head of the bridge. In the poet's words, Majorian's helmet rang under the blows and his cuirass turned the spears until the enemy broke and fled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Scholars have never located Vicus Helena or fixed the battle's date. Edward Gibbon placed it at Lens in 1789, Guilmot at Évin in 1797, and Alexandre-Joseph-Hidulphe Vincent at Allaines near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronne">Péronne</a> in 1840; Hubert le Bourdellès suggested Saint-Amand Abbey and Tony Jaques Hélesmes. On the date, Lanting and van der Plicht argued from Majorian's career for 445 to 450, while Dierkens and Périn dated his victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagaudae">Bagaudae</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, and so the battle, to 448. Alexander O'Hara placed it around 448 at an unidentified site in the Artois.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Vicus Helena” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
